= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**Yes: a deterministic LOCC protocol exists for every allowed value of the parameter.** Here is an explicit implementation, so existence does not depend on merely quoting a pure-state conversion theorem.

First convert the input <Bell state> to $|\Phi^+\rangle=(|00\rangle+|11\rangle)/\sqrt2$ by <local unitary operations>:
$$
(Z_A\otimes X_B)|\Psi^-\rangle=|\Phi^+\rangle.
$$
Write $c=\cos\phi$ and $s=\sin\phi$. Alice performs the two-outcome <quantum measurement> with <Kraus operators>
$$
M_0=\begin{pmatrix}c&0\\0&s\end{pmatrix},\qquad
M_1=\begin{pmatrix}s&0\\0&c\end{pmatrix}.
$$
They define a physical <quantum measurement> because
$$
M_0^\dagger M_0+M_1^\dagger M_1=(c^2+s^2)I=I.
$$
Acting on $|\Phi^+\rangle$, the unnormalized branch vectors are
$$
(M_0\otimes I)|\Phi^+\rangle=\frac{c|00\rangle+s|11\rangle}{\sqrt2},\qquad
(M_1\otimes I)|\Phi^+\rangle=\frac{s|00\rangle+c|11\rangle}{\sqrt2}.
$$
Both branches have <probability> $1/2$. Alice communicates the outcome to Bob. If it is one, both apply the <Pauli X gate>; this turns the normalized second branch into $c|00\rangle+s|11\rangle$. If it is zero, neither applies this correction. Finally Bob applies a <Pauli X gate> in either branch, obtaining
$$
\boxed{c|01\rangle+s|10\rangle.}
$$
Every outcome produces the same target <pure state>, so this is deterministic <local operations and classical communication>, not a successful branch selected by <postselection>. This <two-outcome LOCC dilution of a Bell pair> remains valid at $\phi=0$, where the output is a <product state>, and at $\phi=\pi/4$, where it is another <Bell state>.

The target <Schmidt coefficients> are $c,s$, while those of the input are both $1/\sqrt2$. For an interior value $0<\phi<\pi/4$, <local unitary operations> alone could not change these <Schmidt coefficients>; the local <quantum measurement> is the step that reduces the <entanglement>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Expand an initial bipartite <pure state> in local <orthonormal bases> as
$$
|\psi\rangle=\sum_{i,j}C_{ij}|i\rangle_A|j\rangle_B.
$$
By the <Schmidt decomposition>, or equivalently the <singular value decomposition> of $C$, its <Schmidt rank> is $\operatorname{rank}C$. In a specified local measurement branch, the product <Kraus operator> $A\otimes B$ changes the coefficient <matrix> to
$$
C'=ACB^{\mathsf T}.
$$
Indeed the coefficient of $|k\rangle|l\rangle$ is $\sum_{i,j}A_{ki}C_{ij}B_{lj}$. The elementary <matrix rank> inequality gives
$$
\operatorname{rank}(ACB^{\mathsf T})\leq\operatorname{rank}C.
$$
Normalizing a nonzero branch multiplies $C'$ by a scalar and therefore leaves its <matrix rank> unchanged.

Now refine a complete <LOCC> transcript to record every individual <Kraus operator>. Although later choices depend on the earlier classical outcomes, fixing a transcript fixes all those choices. Composing the local operations therefore gives a single product <Kraus operator> $A_\tau\otimes B_\tau$ in that branch. Each nonzero branch obeys the same <Schmidt-rank contraction under product operators>. Local auxiliary systems prepared independently are included by local isometries and do not add <entanglement>. Discarding a local auxiliary system is included by resolving its <partial trace> in an <orthonormal basis>; this again yields refined product <Kraus operators>.

If the protocol produces a specified <pure state> deterministically, its output <density operator> is a sum of positive rank-one branch <density operators>. Every nonzero branch vector must be proportional to that output vector: its squared overlap with any vector orthogonal to the output sums to zero, so each such overlap is separately zero. Thus the output <Schmidt rank> equals that of each surviving branch, and
$$
\boxed{r_{\mathrm{out}}\leq r_{\mathrm{in}}.}
$$
This proves <monotonicity of Schmidt rank under LOCC>, even for a nonzero branch selected by <postselection>. If the classical transcript is instead discarded and the result is mixed, the refined branch vectors supply an ensemble with <Schmidt ranks> at most $r_{\mathrm{in}}$; by definition its <Schmidt number> is at most $r_{\mathrm{in}}$. This is the correct mixed-state extension, rather than a bound on the ranks of its <reduced density matrices>. In finite dimensions, any limiting exact protocol obeys the same pure-state bound because the set of coefficient <matrices> of rank at most $r_{\mathrm{in}}$ is closed, being defined by vanishing minors.

